description:
Write the request handling of the XenonEcho driver from milestone 2, in plain C++ with a simulated IRP, so it can be tested against every kind of bad input before it ever runs in the VM. In the driver, `dispatch` is called by the dispatch routines; here the tests build the IRPs exactly as the I/O manager would for `METHOD_BUFFERED`.

```cpp
using NTSTATUS = std::int32_t;
constexpr NTSTATUS STATUS_SUCCESS = 0;
constexpr NTSTATUS STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER = static_cast<NTSTATUS>(0xC000000Du);
constexpr NTSTATUS STATUS_INVALID_DEVICE_REQUEST = static_cast<NTSTATUS>(0xC0000010u);
constexpr NTSTATUS STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED = static_cast<NTSTATUS>(0xC0000022u);
constexpr NTSTATUS STATUS_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL = static_cast<NTSTATUS>(0xC0000023u);
enum Major { IRP_MJ_CREATE = 0, IRP_MJ_CLOSE = 2, IRP_MJ_READ = 3, IRP_MJ_WRITE = 4, IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL = 14 };

constexpr std::uint32_t ctl(std::uint32_t fn, std::uint32_t access) { return (0x8000u << 16) | (access << 14) | (fn << 2); }
constexpr std::uint32_t IOCTL_ECHO = ctl(0x800, 0), IOCTL_GET_STATS = ctl(0x801, 1), IOCTL_RESET = ctl(0x802, 2),
                        IOCTL_SET_NAME = ctl(0x803, 2), IOCTL_GET_NAME = ctl(0x804, 1);

struct Irp
{
    int major;
    std::uint32_t code{ 0 };                 // device control only
    std::vector<std::uint8_t> systemBuffer;  // max(inLength, outLength) bytes, input copied in
    std::uint32_t inLength{ 0 }, outLength{ 0 };
    std::uint32_t handleAccess{ 3 };          // bit 0 read, bit 1 write
    NTSTATUS status{ 0 };
    std::uint64_t information{ 0 };
    int completions{ 0 };
};

class EchoDevice
{
public:
    void dispatch(Irp& irp);
    std::uint32_t echoes() const;
    std::uint64_t bytesWritten() const;
    std::u16string name() const;
};
```

**Every IRP is completed exactly once** (set `status` and `information`, and count one completion). On any failure, `information` is 0.

- `IRP_MJ_CREATE` and `IRP_MJ_CLOSE` succeed. `IRP_MJ_WRITE` counts `inLength` bytes towards `bytesWritten` and returns that length as information. Any other major (including read) is `STATUS_INVALID_DEVICE_REQUEST`.
- **Device control**: first the access check the I/O manager would make: if the code's access bits (14-15) are not all in `handleAccess`, `STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED`. Then:
  - `IOCTL_ECHO`: the output must be at least as long as the input (`STATUS_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL` otherwise). The data is already in the system buffer, so echoing is just counting one echo and returning `inLength` as information.
  - `IOCTL_GET_STATS`: needs 16 bytes of output: writes `size` (16, `uint32`), `echoes` (`uint32`) and `bytesWritten` (`uint64`), little-endian, and returns 16.
  - `IOCTL_RESET`: sets both counters to 0.
  - `IOCTL_SET_NAME`: input is a header `{ uint32 size; uint32 length; }` then `length` bytes of UTF-16. Fewer than 8 bytes of input is `STATUS_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL`; `size` must be 8, `length` even, at most 64, and within the input (checked without overflow), else `STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER`. Copy the header once before checking it.
  - `IOCTL_GET_NAME`: writes `uint32 length` then the name's bytes; the output must hold `4 + length` bytes (`STATUS_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL` otherwise), and the information is `4 + length`.
  - Any other code: `STATUS_INVALID_DEVICE_REQUEST`.

